WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.050
If devices sign in with cloud
tokens, why do names still matter?

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.075
Four reasons.

3
00:00:05.075 --> 00:00:07.063
First, Kerberos islands.

4
00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:12.209
Microsoft says an Entra-joined device
signing in to on-premises resources needs

5
00:00:12.209 --> 00:00:17.587
line-of-sight to domain controllers, which
clients find through DNS service records.

6
00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:22.751
And the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 explains that if a name resolves to

7
00:00:22.751 --> 00:00:25.333
the wrong host, a
perfectly valid ticket is

8
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:28.062
presented to the wrong
machine and rejected.

9
00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:29.824
Second, certificates.

10
00:00:29.824 --> 00:00:32.375
A certificate binds a name to a key.

11
00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:34.784
Intune's SCEP certificate profiles,

12
00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:39.923
the protocol Intune uses to request
device certificates, offer a device-name

13
00:00:39.923 --> 00:00:42.814
variable, but the full
domain name variable

14
00:00:42.814 --> 00:00:45.625
is only for Windows domain-joined devices.

15
00:00:45.625 --> 00:00:51.481
As an inference, after AD the certificate's
DNS name has to be built from the device

16
00:00:51.481 --> 00:00:53.754
name plus a fixed agency suffix,

17
00:00:53.754 --> 00:00:57.687
and it only matches DNS if
that same name is registered.

18
00:00:57.687 --> 00:00:58.963
Third, logs.

19
00:00:58.963 --> 00:01:04.050
Book 12 joins every evidence record on
an asset identity from the naming plane.

20
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.263
Microsoft's own log model calls a hostname
used as a device ID a last resort.

21
00:01:09.263 --> 00:01:13.875
As an inference, an unregistered or
duplicated name breaks that join.

22
00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:19.737
Fourth, duplicates: Microsoft says its
naming macros don't guarantee unique names.

23
00:01:19.737 --> 00:01:28.671
Sources: Microsoft Learn: How SSO to
on-premises resources works on Microsoft Entra

24
00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:37.086
joined devices [S55]; Create and assign
SCEP certificate profiles in Intune [S54];

25
00:01:37.086 --> 00:01:44.078
Accounts CSP [S6]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1
About the Client FQDN Option [S41];

26
00:01:44.078 --> 00:01:51.846
Book 08 Ch04 (What Kerberos needs from
DNS); Book 12 Ch02 (evidence contract,

27
00:01:51.846 --> 00:01:56.690
ASIM); Book 15 Ch07 (CMDB
reconcile); Book 07 Ch01.
